    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you provide a technical comparison between Exchange-Traded Funds (ETFs) and Mutual Funds? I specifically need a breakdown of the differences in liquidity, how they are priced throughout the trading day, and the typical management styles (active vs passive) associated with each. A table format would be best.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "| Feature | Exchange-Traded Funds (ETFs) | Mutual Funds |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Liquidity** | High; traded on exchanges like stocks. | Lower; bought or sold directly from the fund company. |\n| **Pricing** | Real-time throughout the trading day based on supply and demand. | Priced once per day at the Net Asset Value (NAV) after market close. |\n| **Management Style** | Often passively managed (tracking an index), though actively managed ETFs exist. | Can be actively managed (fund managers trying to beat the market) or passively managed. |\n\nIn short, ETFs are like a stock you can trade instantly, while mutual funds are like ordering a basket of investments at the end-of-day price. This difference in trading mechanism is key to understanding their operational differences. The management style is variable for both, but ETFs have seen a massive rise in passive index tracking.",
      "source": "target",
